  ],
  "medium": [
   {
    "type": "title",
    "title": "Empire of Brazil",
    "line": "Monarchy in South America",
    "image": "img1"
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "The empire’s government was a representative parliamentary constitutional monarchy under the rule of Emperors Pedro I and his son Pedro II."
   },
   {
    "type": "image",
    "image": "img1",
    "caption": "Empire of Brazil"
   },
   {
    "type": "bignumber",
    "fact": "f74",
    "display": "50,000",
    "caption": "More than 50,000 Brazilian soldiers had died, and war costs were eleven times the government’s annual budget."
   },
   {
    "type": "timeline",
    "events": [
     {
      "fact": "f16",
      "label": "Permanent settlement followed in 1532."
     },
     {
      "fact": "f25",
      "label": "The last Portuguese soldiers to surrender did so in March 1824."
     },
     {
      "fact": "f30",
      "label": "Other difficulties arose when the Empire’s parliament, the General Assembly, opened in 1826."
     },
     {
      "fact": "f39",
      "label": "The Emperor was declared fit to rule in July 1840."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "John VI later returned to Portugal, leaving his eldest son and heir-apparent, Pedro, to rule the Kingdom of Brazil as regent."
   },
   {
    "type": "closing",
    "text": "Lacking any viable heir, the Empire’s political leaders saw no reason to defend the monarchy."
   }
  ],
  "deep": [
   {
    "type": "title",
    "title": "Empire of Brazil",
    "line": "Monarchy in South America",
    "image": "img1"
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "The Empire of Brazil was a 19th-century state that broadly comprised the territories which form modern Brazil and Uruguay until the latter achieved independence in 1828."
   },
   {
    "type": "image",
    "image": "img1",
    "caption": "Empire of Brazil"
   },
   {
    "type": "bignumber",
    "fact": "f17",
    "display": "300 years",
    "caption": "Permanent settlement followed in 1532, and for the next 300 years the Portuguese slowly expanded westwards until they had reached nearly all of the borders of modern Brazil."
   },
   {
    "type": "timeline",
    "events": [
     {
      "fact": "f25",
      "label": "The last Portuguese soldiers to surrender did so in March 1824."
     },
     {
      "fact": "f30",
      "label": "Other difficulties arose when the Empire’s parliament, the General Assembly, opened in 1826."
     },
     {
      "fact": "f39",
      "label": "The Emperor was declared fit to rule in July 1840."
     },
     {
      "fact": "f40",
      "label": "By 1846, Pedro II had matured physically and mentally."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "map",
    "map": "m1",
    "label": "Empire of Brazil"
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "The territory which would come to be known as Brazil was claimed by Portugal on 22 April 1500, when the navigator Pedro Álvares Cabral landed on its coast."
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "In 1808, the army of French Emperor Napoleon I invaded Portugal, forcing the Portuguese royal family—the House of Braganza, a branch of the thousand-year-old Capetian dynasty—into exile."
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "In 1815, the Portuguese crown prince Dom John, acting as regent, created the United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves, which raised the status of Brazil from colony to kingdom."
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "The Portuguese government immediately moved to revoke the political autonomy that Brazil had been granted since 1808."
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "José Bonifácio de Andrada, along with other Brazilian leaders, convinced Pedro to declare Brazil’s independence from Portugal on 7 September 1822."
   },
   {
    "type": "closing",
    "text": "Lacking any viable heir, the Empire’s political leaders saw no reason to defend the monarchy."
   }
  ]
 },
